group(0), start(0), end(0): bob@host 5 13
group(1), start(1), end(1): bob 5 8
m.group() -> group() missing its group index — m.group(0) is the whole match
m.start() -> start() missing its group index — m.start(0) is the whole match
m.end() -> end() missing its group index — m.end(0) is the whole match
m.group(n=1) -> group() takes no keyword arguments
re.sub(p, r, s, 1) -> re.sub() takes 3 argument(s) but 4 were given — CPython's count and flags are not supported
without a count, sub replaces every match: bbb
re.search(p, s, 0) -> re.search() takes 2 argument(s) but 3 were given — CPython's flags argument is not supported; write flags inline in the pattern, as in (?i)
re.fullmatch(p, s, 0) -> re.fullmatch() takes 2 argument(s) but 3 were given — CPython's flags argument is not supported; write flags inline in the pattern, as in (?i)
re.findall(p, s, 0) -> re.findall() takes 2 argument(s) but 3 were given — CPython's flags argument is not supported; write flags inline in the pattern, as in (?i)
re.finditer(p, s, 0) -> re.finditer() takes 2 argument(s) but 3 were given — CPython's flags argument is not supported; write flags inline in the pattern, as in (?i)
re.split(p, s, 1) -> re.split() takes 2 argument(s) but 3 were given — CPython's maxsplit and flags are not supported
re.compile(p, 0) -> re.compile() takes 1 argument(s) but 2 were given — CPython's flags argument is not supported; write flags inline in the pattern, as in (?i)
a flag goes inline instead: a
p.search(s, 1) -> Pattern.search() takes 1 argument(s) but 2 were given — CPython's pos and endpos are not supported
p.fullmatch(s, 0) -> Pattern.fullmatch() takes 1 argument(s) but 2 were given — CPython's pos and endpos are not supported
p.findall(s, 1) -> Pattern.findall() takes 1 argument(s) but 2 were given — CPython's pos and endpos are not supported
p.finditer(s, 1) -> Pattern.finditer() takes 1 argument(s) but 2 were given — CPython's pos and endpos are not supported
p.split(s, 1) -> Pattern.split() takes 1 argument(s) but 2 were given — CPython's maxsplit is not supported
p.sub(r, s, 1) -> Pattern.sub() takes 2 argument(s) but 3 were given — CPython's count is not supported
the forms Oro takes still answer: ['a', 'b', 'c'] a;b;c
re.sub(p, r, s, count=1) -> re.sub() takes no keyword arguments
